# TODO

The work list with implementation details. The overview for people is in
README.md, the rules are in CONTRIBUTING.md.

Priority says how important an item is for a usable window manager, not when
it will be done. The order inside a section is the order of work; `after:`
names a dependency. Every item needs a test (see CONTRIBUTING.md).

## Must

Window management that other programs can rely on.

### Repository and quality tooling (first, one small commit per step)

The goal is to be as strict as the tools allow and to let the tools, not the
reviewer, keep it that way. Order of work:

1. ~~Tidy the root~~ (done: `old/` and the stray files are gone, the
   experiments `keys.cc`, `xkeyexample.c` and the `blackbox` sources were
   moved out of the repository to `../tiny-shell-attic/`).
2. ~~New layout~~ (done: `src/`, `examples/`, `tools/`, `build/`).
3. **Local configuration is ignored.** `tools/run-xephyr.sh` uses
   `local/xinitrc` (git ignored) and creates it from `examples/xinitrc` when
   it does not exist, so each machine can tune the screen size, the scale
   and the applications without touching the repository. The Makefile
   includes `local/config.mk` when it exists (e.g. a different `CXX`).
   Done, except for a test of the script on another machine.
4. ~~Strict warnings~~ (done: the long list of warnings is in the
   Makefile, the sources are clean with GCC 16 and Clang 19, 21, 22;
   `make STRICT=1` makes the warnings errors, CI will use it).
5. ~~Clang by default~~ (done: `clang++`, else the newest `clang++-N`, else
   `g++`). Still open: `-D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=3`, `-D_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS` and
   `-fstack-protector-strong` for release builds.
6. **Formatting and static analysis.** Done: `.clang-format` (the style of
   the existing code), `make format`, one formatting-only commit listed in
   `.git-blame-ignore-revs` (`git config blame.ignoreRevsFile
   .git-blame-ignore-revs`). Still open: `.clang-tidy` and `make lint`
   (clang-tidy is not installed on the development machine yet).
   Plan for the rest: `.clang-tidy` (`bugprone-*`, `clang-analyzer-*`, `cert-*`,
   `cppcoreguidelines-*` with the noisy checks listed and explained,
   `misc-*`, `modernize-*`, `performance-*`, `readability-*`).
   `WarningsAsErrors: '*'`. Targets `make format`, `make lint` (clang-tidy
   with `compile_commands.json`, generated instead of `compile_flags.txt`)
   and `make cppcheck` (optional, a second opinion).
7. **pre-commit.** `.pre-commit-config.yaml` with: `clang-format`,
   whitespace and end-of-file fixers, merge-conflict and large-file checks,
   `shellcheck` and `shfmt` for `tools/`, `codespell`, a check that the file
   names stay lower case. Fast checks run on commit; `clang-tidy` and
   `make check` run in the `pre-push` stage, since they take long. CI runs
   `pre-commit run --all-files`, so the hooks and CI cannot disagree.
8. **Sanitizers.** `make asan` (`-fsanitize=address,undefined
   -fno-sanitize-recover=all`) runs the whole `make check`. The leaks of the
   toolkit that are accepted for now go to one suppression file with a
   comment each, not to a skipped test.
9. **GitHub workflow** (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`; a matrix, all jobs run
   `make check` under Xvfb with `STRICT=1`):
   - Debian stable with GCC and with Clang (the latest and the oldest
     version that supports C++17 we promise);
   - Clang with `libc++`;
   - sanitizers: ASan + UBSan (the code is single threaded, TSan is not
     needed);
   - **Alpine (musl)** in a container, GCC and Clang: musl is what small
     embedded systems really use, so this is the most valuable "other
     platform";
   - FreeBSD (and later OpenBSD) with `cross-platform-actions`, build and
     tests, since BSD is claimed in the README;
   - `pre-commit` job (format, lint, shellcheck);
   - coverage with `gcovr`, which fails below a threshold (a ratchet: the
     number only goes up, we do not chase 100 %), the report is an artifact;
   - size job: prints `size` of `tiny-shell` and warns when it grows by
     more than a set amount; "small" is a goal, so it is measured;
   - Dependabot for the versions of the actions.
10. **Document it.** Update CONTRIBUTING.md (Layout, Development setup,
    Tests) with the final commands as each step lands; `make help` lists
    the targets.

### Code cleanup

- `Window::set_minimized`, `set_maximized`, `set_focus`, `return_focus` do
  `&(*atoms.begin())` on a vector that can be empty: undefined behavior. Use
  `atoms.data()`. The four copies of the `XChangeProperty(_NET_WM_STATE)`
  block are one function `Window::publish_wm_states()`.
- `Window::get_properties` prints every property name with `printf`; remove or
  put it behind `DEBUG`.
- `main.cc`: print the reason of the failure (`e.what()`) and take
  `wm_detected` out of the global namespace.
- Segfaults: find where a null `tiny::display` / null `XftFont` can be
  dereferenced (`get_display()` already aborts; check `Style::get_font()`
  when the font is not found) and turn it into an error message at startup,
  or into a compile time error where it is possible.

### Manage one window in one place

- `Manager` constructor (the `XQueryTree` loop) and `Manager::on_map_request`
  contain the same code: window type check, Motif hints, `Window::create` or
  `Wrapped::create`, registration in `wm_windows`, `wm_tops`, `wm_dock` and
  the `on_focus` signal. Extract `Window* Manager::manage(::Window child,
  const XWindowAttributes&)` and `bool Manager::is_managed_type(::Window)`.
- The start-up scan must also skip windows that are `override_redirect` or
  `IsUnmapped`; keep it, it is correct.
- Do not lock a decision in the constructor: `Window::get_motif_hints` leaves
  `functions` and `decorations` uninitialized when it returns false; set the
  defaults (`FUNC_ALL`, `DECOR_ALL`) inside it.

### Window life cycle

- Handle `DestroyNotify` of a client. Today only `UnmapNotify` removes the
  window, and `on_unmap_notify` returns early for a minimized window, so a
  minimized window that is destroyed stays in the dock.
- Tell an unmap done by the manager (minimize, switching desktops later) from
  an unmap done by the client (withdraw). Count the expected unmaps in
  `Window` (the ICCCM way) instead of the `get_minimized()` shortcut.
- Set `WM_STATE` (`NormalState`, `IconicState`, `WithdrawnState`) on the
  client; ICCCM clients and session tools read it. Remove `_NET_WM_STATE` and
  `WM_STATE` when the window is withdrawn.
- Put the client windows into the save set (`XAddToSaveSet`) so they survive
  a crash of the manager, and reparent them back to the root in `~Manager`
  (the frame removal already does it, check the exit path too).
- When a client dies while the manager reads its properties, `BadWindow` must
  be ignored. `Manager::on_error` only logs; make it quiet for
  `BadWindow`/`BadDrawable`/`BadMatch` on request codes of the managed
  windows.

### ConfigureRequest and geometry

- `Manager::on_configure_request` calls `XConfigureWindow` on the client
  window, ignoring the frame. For a `Wrapped` window the request must go to
  `Wrapped`, which resizes its frame (`header` and `wm_win_border` included)
  and the client together. Route it by `wm_windows`.
- After every move or resize of a framed client, send the synthetic
  `ConfigureNotify` with root coordinates to the client (ICCCM 4.1.5).
- Honour `PWinGravity` and the position hints (`USPosition`, `PPosition`) when
  placing a new window; today the attributes are taken as they are. Place
  windows without a position hint under the panel, not over it.
- Resizing: respect `PMinSize`, `PMaxSize`, `PBaseSize`, `PResizeInc` and
  `PAspect`. The increment is done; the aspect ratio only logs
  `Aspect ratio problem...` (`Window::Window`, `wm_window.cc`).
- `Window::maximize` / `Window::restore` ask the root window for the screen
  size (`TODO: get screen size instead`). Add `Manager::work_area()` and give
  it to windows (see struts below).

### EWMH

All of this lives in a new `wm_ewmh.{h,cc}` that the `Manager` owns; the
atoms are already in `display.h`, add the missing ones there
(`_NET_SUPPORTING_WM_CHECK`, `_NET_CLIENT_LIST`, `_NET_CLIENT_LIST_STACKING`,
`_NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW`, `_NET_WORKAREA`, `_NET_NUMBER_OF_DESKTOPS`,
`_NET_CURRENT_DESKTOP`, `_NET_DESKTOP_GEOMETRY`, `_NET_CLOSE_WINDOW`,
`_NET_WM_STRUT`, `_NET_WM_STRUT_PARTIAL`, `_NET_FRAME_EXTENTS`,
`_NET_REQUEST_FRAME_EXTENTS`, `WM_TRANSIENT_FOR`).

- Enable `Manager::set_supported()` (it is commented out in the constructor)
  and list only what is really implemented, so applications are not lied to.
  Create the `_NET_SUPPORTING_WM_CHECK` child window with `_NET_WM_NAME`
  `tiny-shell` on it and on the root.
- Keep `_NET_CLIENT_LIST`, `_NET_CLIENT_LIST_STACKING` and
  `_NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW` up to date on every manage, unmanage and focus change.
  One `Manager::publish_ewmh()` called from those places is enough.
- Client messages on the root window: `_NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW`,
  `_NET_CLOSE_WINDOW`, `_NET_WM_STATE`, `WM_CHANGE_STATE` (iconify, used by
  `XIconifyWindow`). Today `Window::on_client_message` knows only the
  maximize add/remove pair, the rest logs "Unhandled". It reads the client
  window events, but EWMH clients send these to the root; connect
  `ClientMessage` on the root in `Manager`.
- `_NET_WM_STATE`: add toggle, fullscreen, above, below, shaded, skip
  taskbar, demands attention. Handle the two property atoms of a request
  (`data.l[1]` and `data.l[2]`) independently; the code now accepts the
  maximize pair only when both come together.
- Fullscreen: no frame, size of the monitor, above the panel; restore the
  old state after.
- Struts: read `_NET_WM_STRUT_PARTIAL` of docks, compute the work area, set
  `_NET_WORKAREA`, and make `maximize` use it. The own panel sets its strut
  in the same way (an in-process dock is just a dock). after: work area.
- Transient windows: read `WM_TRANSIENT_FOR`, keep a transient above its
  parent, close/minimize it with the parent, place a dialog over the parent.
  Honour `_NET_WM_STATE_MODAL` (focus goes to the dialog).
- Window types: `DIALOG`, `UTILITY`, `TOOLBAR`, `MENU` get fewer buttons or no
  dock entry; `DESKTOP` is kept at the bottom, `DOCK` is not framed (done).
- `_NET_FRAME_EXTENTS`: set it on the client after framing.

### Event loop and children

- Replace `XNextEvent` in `Manager::main_loop` with `poll()` on
  `ConnectionNumber(display)`, a `timerfd` and a self-pipe/`signalfd`. Before
  `poll`, drain `XPending`. Timers are needed for the clock and for the
  popover close delay (`Popover::time_from_unmap`).
- Handle `SIGCHLD`: reap children with `waitpid(-1, WNOHANG)` in the loop (or
  `SA_NOCLDWAIT`). `RunDialog` uses `fork` + `execl("/bin/sh")` without
  anything that collects the child: zombies. Also `setsid()` in the child so a
  program started from the shell does not die with it.
- `SIGTERM`/`SIGHUP` end the loop cleanly (like the logout item does).
- Make Restart and Shutdown in `UserMenu` do something or hide them: only
  logout is connected. Both should run a configurable command (default
  `systemctl reboot` / `poweroff`, none when the command is empty).

### Event dispatch

- `tiny::Handlers` stores `void (Object::*)(const XEvent&, void*)` and the
  callers `reinterpret_cast` member functions of derived classes
  (`wm_window.cc` `set_events`, `Window::set_events`). `Wrapped` inherits
  `Container` and `Window`, and both derive from `Object` without `virtual`,
  so there are two `Object` sub-objects and the `this` pointer a handler gets
  depends on which one was registered. Replace with
  `std::function<void(const XEvent&)>` and lambdas:
  - `Handlers::set_handler(type, window, std::function)`; keep the
    `(type, window)` key.
  - `Object::connect_window` takes the function; the destructor still
    unregisters everything (RAII, keep the `connected_events` set).
  - `Signal` keeps its job (widget to owner) with a `std::function` inside;
    `connect(this, method, data)` becomes `connect([this](...){...})`.
  - Do it widget by widget, keep both APIs for a while; the tests of each
    widget are the safety net.
  - after: nothing, but do it before adding new widgets.
- `Handlers::call_hanlder` has a typo in its name; rename on the way.
- Drop `Object` as a base class of `Window` once nothing needs it
  (`Wrapped` then inherits one `Object` only through `Container`).

## Should

### Panel and shell

- Clock: new `ClockLabel` in the panel center (or right); format
  `%a %d. %m. %H:%M` by `strftime` with the locale; redraw by timer at the
  start of every minute. after: event loop with timers.
- Task bar: a `TaskBar` widget in the panel that shows one button per window
  (icon + title, ellipsized), highlights the focused one, click focuses or
  minimizes. Same data as the dock (`Manager::wm_dock.add/remove`); introduce
  `WindowList` (a model that notifies about add/remove/focus/title change)
  and let both the dock and the task bar be its views. The panel layout
  (task bar in the panel, dock at the bottom, or both) comes from the
  configuration.
- Update titles and icons live: `PropertyNotify` of `_NET_WM_NAME`,
  `_NET_WM_ICON` is read, check that the dock/task bar get a redraw.
- Alt+Tab order: today `wm_tops` keeps the creation order. Make it MRU:
  move the window to the end of `wm_tops` in `Manager::on_window_focus`;
  `activate_next_window` / `activate_prev_window` walk it. Keep
  `_NET_CLIENT_LIST_STACKING` in the stacking order.
- Alt+Tab with a visible switcher while Alt is held (grab the keyboard,
  commit on release). Today it switches on key release only.
- Drawing: `on_expose` creates and frees a `GC` each time. Keep one `GC` per
  widget (or per display) and draw into a `Pixmap`, then `XCopyArea`, to stop
  the flicker of hover in the panel and the dock. `Widget` gets
  `begin_paint()`/`end_paint()`.
- Fonts: `Style::font_family` is `"Cantarell"`; use a fontconfig fallback
  list (`"Cantarell,DejaVu Sans,sans"`) so an embedded system without the
  font still has readable text. Report `TODO: check success` in
  `Theme::init` as an error.

### Configuration

- A tiny ini file `~/.config/tiny-shell/config` (`XDG_CONFIG_HOME`), parsed
  by a ~100 line parser without dependencies. Sections: `[keys]`
  (`Alt+Tab = next-window`), `[panel]` (layout, clock format), `[commands]`
  (terminal, logout, reboot, poweroff), `[theme]` (font, scale).
- Action registry: `ActionRegistry` maps a name to a `std::function`
  (`close-window`, `maximize`, `minimize`, `next-window`, `prev-window`,
  `run-dialog`, `launcher`, `exec:<command>`, `logout`). The key table of
  `Manager::set_events` / `on_key_press` / `on_key_release` (now hard coded
  Alt+Tab, Alt+Shift+Tab, Alt+F2) becomes a table of bindings parsed from the
  configuration, using `XStringToKeysym`. Menus and later Lua use the same
  registry. This is the foundation for scripting.
- Respect the keyboard layout changes: re-grab the keys on `MappingNotify`.

### freedesktop.org: desktop entries and launcher

- `DesktopEntry` parser (`lib/desktop_entry.{h,cc}`, no X inside, fully unit
  tested): `[Desktop Entry]` group, `Type=Application`, `Name` with
  `Name[cs_CZ]`/`Name[cs]` fallback by `LANG`, `GenericName`, `Comment`,
  `Exec` with field codes (`%f %F %u %U` removed, `%i %c %k` expanded,
  `%%`), `Icon`, `Terminal`, `Categories`, `NoDisplay`, `Hidden`,
  `OnlyShowIn`/`NotShowIn` (`tiny-shell` is not a desktop name; treat as
  unknown), `TryExec`.
- `ApplicationIndex`: scan `$XDG_DATA_HOME/applications` and every
  `$XDG_DATA_DIRS/applications` (with subdirectories, desktop file id by
  path), earlier directories win; cache in memory, rescan on `inotify`
  (`IN_CREATE|IN_DELETE|IN_MODIFY`), as one more fd in the poll loop.
- Application menu (`AppMenu`, today empty): group entries by the main
  categories (`AudioVideo`, `Development`, `Education`, `Game`, `Graphics`,
  `Network`, `Office`, `Settings`, `System`, `Utility`); do not implement
  `applications.menu` (XML merge rules), see the ideas at the end.
- Launch: `fork` + `setsid` + `execvp`/`sh -c` with the expanded `Exec`;
  `Terminal=true` runs the configured terminal. One function used by the menu,
  the launcher and the run dialog.
- Launcher (`Super` or a panel button): an overlay window with the existing
  `Entry` on top and a `ListBox` below (new widget: items with icon and
  text, keyboard up/down/enter, scrolling with `Button4/5`). Search is a
  case- and accent-insensitive substring/prefix match over `Name`,
  `GenericName`, `Keywords`, `Exec`; ranking: prefix of a word first, then
  substring, then recently used (a small `~/.cache/tiny-shell/recent` file).
  The ranking function is pure and unit tested.
- Run dialog (`wm_run.cc`): `TODO: suggest like command line`; complete
  commands from `$PATH` with Tab. Position the dialog on the monitor center
  (`TODO: working with position`).

### Icons

- `IconTheme`: look up `Icon=` names by the spec — absolute path as is; else
  `hicolor` (and the theme from the configuration) by size directories
  (`index.theme` `Directories`, `Size`, `Type`), then `/usr/share/pixmaps`;
  extensions `.png`, `.xpm`; no SVG. Cache by `(name, size)`.
- PNG decoding: vendor `stb_image.h` in `lib/third_party/` (public domain),
  convert to premultiplied ARGB, scale with the existing `resize_pixmap`
  (Xrender transform) to `theme.wm_icon`. Document it in CONTRIBUTING.md
  Dependencies.
- ARGB icons: `_NET_WM_ICON` is converted to a `Pixmap` + mask. Keep an
  ARGB `Picture` and composite with `XRenderComposite` for smooth alpha
  instead of a 1-bit mask (`Icon::on_expose`, `TODO: move to wm_window`).
- Use `_GTK_*`/`StartupWMClass` only if needed for matching windows to
  entries; first match by `WM_CLASS` to the `StartupWMClass` or the entry
  name so that the task bar shows the entry icon when a window has none.

### Quality

- Tests of the manager in `tests/test-wm-manager.cc` with fake clients
  (`tests/client.h`) for: destroy while minimized, configure request of a
  framed window, transient dialogs, struts, fullscreen toggle, every
  `_NET_WM_STATE` message.
- Measure and write down in README: binary size (`-Os`, `-s`), resident
  memory after start, with and without windows. Add a size check to CI
  later. Try `-ffunction-sections -fdata-sections -Wl,--gc-sections`.
- Screen instead of `0`: `XDefaultDepth(dsp, 0)` and friends
  (`wm_window.cc`, `x_util.cc`); take the screen from the root window of the
  manager.
- Replace the `TODO` comments in the code that duplicate items here with a
  link to the item (`containers.cc`: popover must stay on the root,
  `wm_dock.cc`: the click must end on the button, `wm_wrapped.cc`: set state,
  restore inside the desktop).

## Could

- Multiple monitors: RandR (`libXrandr`, optional at build time) for the
  geometry of the monitors, `_NET_WORKAREA` per monitor, maximize and
  fullscreen on the monitor of the window, the panel on the primary one.
  Today the whole root window is used everywhere.
- Virtual desktops: `_NET_NUMBER_OF_DESKTOPS`, `_NET_CURRENT_DESKTOP`,
  `_NET_WM_DESKTOP`, sticky windows; pager in the panel. after: EWMH.
- System tray: `_NET_SYSTEM_TRAY_S0` selection owner, XEMBED docking of
  icons in the panel, `_NET_SYSTEM_TRAY_OPCODE`. It is a lot of protocol for
  a few icons; do it when someone needs it.
- Lua scripting (optional build, `make LUA=1`, Lua 5.4): a `tiny` module with
  the action registry (`tiny.bind("Mod4+t", "exec:xterm")`), window events
  (`on_manage`, `on_focus`, `on_title`), and read-only window information.
  No access to Xlib from Lua. The script runs in the main loop and must not
  block it. after: action registry, configuration.
- Window snapping to the edges of the screen and to other windows while
  moving; half-screen maximize (Super+Left/Right).
- Shaded windows (roll up by double click on the title bar); `_NET_WM_STATE_SHADED`
  is already in the state table.
- Session management / saving the layout.
- Focus follows mouse as a configuration option (click to focus is the
  default).
- Use `_NET_WM_PING` to find hung applications and offer to kill them
  instead of `XKillClient` right away.
- Startup notification (`_NET_STARTUP_ID`, the `startup-notification`
  protocol is client messages only, no library needed).
- Cursors for edges and corners are done; add a configurable cursor theme
  through `Xcursor` only if it stays optional.

## Ideas that are not decided

Things to discuss before they go to a section above.

- Run the shell (panel, dock, launcher) as a separate process that uses only
  EWMH. It makes the window manager smaller and crash-proof, and tint2 and
  others could replace it, but the second process costs memory. Keep the
  boundary clean (CONTRIBUTING.md, Architecture) and decide when EWMH is
  complete.
- `applications.menu` (Desktop Menu Specification): leaning to "no", the
  category grouping is enough.
- SVG icons: leaning to "no" (needs a rasterizer, i.e. a dependency).
- Wayland / a compositor: out of scope (README). If any of these gets a
  reason to return, record it in `wont.md` with the condition.
